Farai owns a hardware shop in Chinhoyi. His bookkeeper, Thandeka, prepared a net profit of US\$14,260 for the year ended 31 December 2025. Farai then asked her to:

1. leave out cash sales of US\$2,750 "so that the tax will be lower";
2. continue recording his wife's monthly allowance, US\$1,200 for the year, as wages;
3. tell him the salaries of the staff at a rival shop, which she also keeps books for.

Tatenda manages a building supplies company in Bulawayo and is applying for a bank loan. The accounts he sent to the bank show a profit of US\$21,500 for the year ended 31 December 2025. The company's accountant, Rudo, discovers that:

- sales of US\$3,800 were invented by Tatenda;
- a customer who owes US\$950 has been declared bankrupt, but the debt was not written off.
